      <h1 id="nbde-about-disk-encryption-technology" class="group scroll-mt-20">About disk encryption technology<a href="#nbde-about-disk-encryption-technology" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Network-Bound Disk Encryption (NBDE) allows you to encrypt root volumes of hard drives on physical and virtual machines without having to manually enter a password when restarting machines.</p>
      <h2 id="nbde-disk-encryption-technology-comparison_nbde-implementation" class="group scroll-mt-20">Disk encryption technology comparison<a href="#nbde-disk-encryption-technology-comparison_nbde-implementation" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>To understand the merits of Network-Bound Disk Encryption (NBDE) for securing data at rest on edge servers, compare key escrow and TPM disk encryption without Clevis to NBDE on systems running Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL).</p>
      <p>The following table presents some tradeoffs to consider around the threat model and the complexity of each encryption solution.</p>
